101 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 71 0 R
/E (I was watching the sunset over the ocean from the cape, near the lighthouse that had just been remodeled.  And as the light turned the fields golden and the shadows stretched from the forest, I was thinking about the urge to get out, away from the hustle and bustle of moving to a new city.  How often do we take a step back to appreciate the landscape and natural environment?)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
102 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 71 0 R
/E (To that end, I immediately packed my bags, booked a flight, and departed to unknown biomes, camera in hand....)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
103 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 71 0 R
/E (And what better place to experience the dichotomy between manmade and natural than the harsh, unforgiving badlands, a land almost alien to the familiar mechanized world?)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
104 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 71 0 R
/E (Roaming the red, sun-baked sand, is the small, three-banded armadillo. These little guys are the natural enemy to spiders, making the badlands safe from at least one mob at night and the armadillo a natural companion to miners venturing down into abandoned mineshafts that riddle the biome.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
105 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 71 0 R
/E (Three-banded armadillos are also the only armadillo species that can curl into a perfectly square ball!)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
106 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 71 0 R
/E (As I was observing this armadillo colony, I turned around, and there on the shore, was a sight so unexpected, I almost couldn't realize what I was seeing! With an inverted palette that looked like a black skeleton against the harsh sand, was what some say is the rarest canine on the planet: the striped wolf.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
107 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 71 0 R
/E (There was only one.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
108 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 71 0 R
/E (The snowy wolf is often thought to hold that designation, but a desertifying environment and habitat loss make the striped wolf's native wooded badlands a vanishing biome. In fact, there was not a tree in sight, indicating that this solo individual had traveled far in search of prey, finding it among this plentiful armadillo population. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
109 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 71 0 R
/E (But armadillos being the armored prey that they are, this fellow had taken to chasing the local desert chickens in search of a meal.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
110 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 71 0 R
/E (I followed him for several days, and although he was aware of my presence, he didn't seem to think I was a threat. Photography was another matter - unlike the slow armadillos, he never sat still for long; no sooner had he settled and I steadied my camera, he would be off and running, as if he wanted to remain an enigmatic grey ghost.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [18]
>>
